19. Performance and resource protection

The system must operate reliably on the target VPS.

Use bounded resource consumption across all background work.

Requirements:

Limit concurrent ingestion jobs.

Prevent duplicate scheduled work.

Limit active market-data subscriptions.

Use provider-aware polling.

Reuse existing connections where appropriate.

Avoid unbounded queues.

Limit response sizes.

Paginate logs and execution history.

Avoid rendering huge operational tables at once.

Apply retention and log rotation.

Use database indexes for frequent queries.

Use caching where beneficial.

Shut down cleanly on termination signals.

Avoid synchronous expensive operations in request handlers.

Ensure optional AI workloads cannot starve essential ingestion or user-facing API requests.

If resource monitoring is unavailable, state that limitation rather than displaying invented CPU or memory values.

Do not create a system that assumes every exchange, news source, AI provider, and external alerting integration is active simultaneously.

20. Testing requirements

Add tests appropriate to the existing stack.

At minimum, validate:

Authentication and authorization

Anonymous users cannot access administrative routes.

Anonymous users cannot call administrative APIs.

Operators cannot perform actions beyond their permissions.

Sensitive settings are never returned in full.

Invalid or expired sessions are handled safely.

Source and provider management

Invalid configuration is rejected.

Disabled sources do not run.

Connection-test failures are recorded.

Rate limits are handled.

Credentials are not logged.

Unsafe source URLs are rejected.

Missing credentials do not create false connected states.

Scheduler and jobs

Jobs do not overlap when overlap is prohibited.

Failed jobs receive bounded retries.

Permanent failures do not retry forever.

Manual execution requires authorization.

Shutdown does not leave uncontrolled background work.

Execution history is persisted correctly.

Data quality and persistence

Invalid records are flagged appropriately.

Duplicate processing does not create duplicate records.

Retention jobs obey the configured policy.

Migrations can be applied on a test database.

Backup verification does not modify production data.

Deployment and operational behavior

Health endpoints report the correct state.

Required configuration is validated.

Optional integrations can remain disabled.

Public responses do not expose internal stack traces.

Logs do not contain secrets.

The application starts without an AI key when AI is optional.

Run the actual build, type checks, lint checks, tests, and migration checks available in the repository.

Record the results. Do not claim success without running the commands.

21. Implementation sequence

Work in the following order.

Stage 1 — Repository audit

Document the actual stack, current features, integrations, and gaps.

Stage 2 — Operational data model

Create only the required database models, migrations, and service interfaces.

Stage 3 — Protected Control Center

Build authenticated routes, authorization, overview, and module navigation.

Stage 4 — Connector management

Integrate real source and provider health, configuration validation, safe testing, and diagnostics.

Stage 5 — Background jobs

Implement or repair scheduling, execution tracking, bounded retries, and manual operations.

Stage 6 — Data quality and observability

Implement health checks, operational logs, quality findings, alerts, and audit history.

Stage 7 — Backup and deployment

Prepare environment templates, startup configuration, process supervision, reverse proxy guidance, backup and restore instructions.

Stage 8 — Security review

Review authorization, secret handling, SSRF exposure, unsafe content, logs, public endpoints, and administrative operations.

Stage 9 — Validation

Run tests and production build checks. Fix implementation-caused failures and document remaining blockers.

Do not implement every stage as a mockup and then declare the system complete. Prioritize the smallest complete operational path and expand it incrementally.

22. Acceptance criteria

This phase is complete only when:

The existing public application remains intact.

The Control Center is protected by real server-side authorization.

Administrative APIs enforce permissions independently of the UI.

The overview reflects actual system and dependency health.

Source and provider statuses are based on real configuration and operational results.

Credentials are never exposed in frontend bundles or ordinary diagnostic responses.

Background jobs have observable execution history.

Retries are bounded and permanent failures are handled correctly.

Manual operations require authorization.

Data-quality issues can be inspected.

Health endpoints distinguish process liveness from dependency readiness.

Logs and audit records avoid secret leakage.

Backup and restoration instructions match the actual database.

Deployment instructions match the actual project and runtime.

Optional integrations can remain disabled without breaking the application.

Resource consumption is bounded and appropriate for the target VPS.

AdSense configuration is reported honestly.

Administrative routes are not treated as public monetized content.

Security-relevant tests are included and executed.

Build, lint, type checks, migrations, and tests are run where available.

Missing configuration and unsupported features are explicitly documented.

No fake operational success states remain.

